{
  "code": "us_manufacturer",
  "name": "US manufacturer",
  "description": "The US small business chart for a company that makes what it sells, in USD: raw materials and purchased goods (1300, the default inventory account) kept apart from finished goods (1310, to be set on the items or warehouse that hold them), goods in transit between sites (1320), the labor and overhead a production order absorbs at standard (5050, a credit against cost) and its variance against actual (5060), payment differences written off (6160), and unrealized exchange gains and losses from revaluing open items (4930, 6170) kept apart from the realized ones. Work in progress has no account: a production order takes its materials and books what it makes when it is completed. The rest is as in the US small business template: A chart of accounts for a small business in the United States that buys and sells goods: assets, liabilities, equity, income, cost of goods sold and expenses, in US dollars, with a calendar fiscal year. Sales tax in the US depends on the jurisdiction of the buyer and is beyond this template: it creates one flat code, US-SALES-TAX, at 0 %, so invoices can carry a code; set its rate, or add codes per jurisdiction, before relying on it. It also shows the shape of a sale taxed by a state and a county at once: the group US-EXAMPLE-STATE-COUNTY of US-EXAMPLE-STATE (4 %) and US-EXAMPLE-COUNTY (1.5 %), each owed on its own account. These are examples, not the rates of any real state or county: replace them with your jurisdictions' codes and rates.",
  "country": "US",
  "currency": "USD",
  "start_month": 1,
  "sources": [
    "Manufacturing accounts 1310, 1320, 4930, 5050, 5060, 6160 and 6170 are numbered by this template within the small-business chart's ranges; they follow no published standard. Rename or renumber them to your own chart."
